WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.403
Authentication and authorization are related, but they are not the same
control.

2
00:00:04.403 --> 00:00:07.465
Authentication verifies who or what is making a request.

3
00:00:07.465 --> 00:00:14.101
Authorization decides whether that authenticated subject is allowed to
perform a specific action on a specific resource.

4
00:00:14.101 --> 00:00:18.248
A common mistake is treating a logged-in session as permission to do too
much.

5
00:00:18.248 --> 00:00:25.522
Another is checking for a broad administrator flag when the real decision
should be narrower and tied to the operation being requested.

6
00:00:25.522 --> 00:00:28.521
Object-level authorization is especially important.

7
00:00:28.521 --> 00:00:38.857
A Python API may check that a user is authenticated before loading a
record, file, project, tenant, or report, but still fail to verify that
this user is allowed to access that particular object.

8
00:00:38.857 --> 00:00:41.218
Tenant boundaries need the same attention.

9
00:00:41.218 --> 00:00:49.896
A user from one organization should not be able to reach another
organization's data simply because an identifier, route, or background job
path accepts a value.

10
00:00:49.896 --> 00:00:56.149
Client-side checks, hidden buttons, and hidden routes are usability
features, not sufficient security controls.

11
00:00:56.149 --> 00:01:00.615
Clients can be modified, requests can be replayed, and APIs can be called
directly.

12
00:01:00.615 --> 00:01:04.571
Sensitive checks should be enforced server-side and close to the action.

13
00:01:04.571 --> 00:01:13.823
Export operations, bulk updates, administrative tools, background jobs,
and support workflows need authorization review just as much as ordinary
user interface paths.

14
00:01:13.823 --> 00:01:16.439
Session behavior also creates subtle mistakes.

15
00:01:16.439 --> 00:01:25.435
Unsafe assumptions about session lifetime, account recovery, role changes,
token handling, logout, and device trust can undermine otherwise sound
access control.

16
00:01:25.435 --> 00:01:29.391
Tokens and session identifiers should be handled as sensitive material.

17
00:01:29.391 --> 00:01:35.070
When privileges change, the application should have a clear story for what
happens to existing sessions.

18
00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:42.280
Every sensitive action should verify whether the current caller is allowed
to perform that specific operation at that specific time.
